  "medium": {
    "heading": "Finding the Balance in the Middle",
    "body": "The medium puzzle today, designed by Rodolfo Kurchan, really stepped things up with those equals constraints! Unlike the easy mode where we just add things up, the equals regions require you to find dominoes that share the same values or can be grouped to match. I spent a good few minutes staring at the large equals region covering [0,2], [0,3], [1,2], and [1,3]. When you have four cells that all must be equal, you have to look at your available dominoes for duplicates.\n\nI noticed we had a [5,5], a [0,0], and a [1,1] domino in the list. This was a huge hint! I realized that the equals region at [0,0] and [0,1] had to be solved alongside the bigger ones. By placing the [5,5] and [4,5] dominoes strategically, I was able to satisfy the equals constraint for the right side of the board. It felt like a game of musical chairs trying to make sure every cell in a region had the exact same number of pips.\n\nThe breakthrough came when I looked at the less than 3 constraint at cell [3,2]. This limited my options significantly. Once I placed the [3,0] domino across [2,0] and [3,0], the rest of the bottom section started to make sense. Working with the [2,1] and [1,1] dominoes near the empty cell at [1,1] helped me seal the deal. It was definitely a step up from the easy puzzle, but so rewarding once the logic clicked.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "Conquering the Greater Than Grids",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today was a real workout! Rodolfo Kurchan certainly didn't make it easy for us. The standout feature here was that greater than 9 constraint at [2,4] and [3,4]. In a game using standard dominoes, a sum greater than 9 is quite rare. I immediately looked at the [4,6] domino because it is one of the few that actually adds up to 10. That piece became the anchor for my entire solution.\n\nI hit a bit of a dead end early on trying to figure out the sum 9 regions at the top and left. With dominoes like [2,5] and [0,3], I had to be very careful about which numbers I used to reach those high totals. I eventually realized that cell [1,0] and [2,0] needing a sum of 9 meant I had to use the [4,5] or similar high-value sides. The placement of the [6,0] domino was also a bit of a head-scratcher until I saw how it could satisfy the less than 3 constraint near the bottom left at [3,0] and [4,0].\n\nThe real hero of this puzzle was the equals constraint at [4,3] and [4,4]. By linking them with the [4,2] domino, I was finally able to balance the bottom of the board. This one required a lot of back-and-forth, but seeing that final grid filled out was the perfect way to finish my morning session. If you are struggling with this one, focus on those high-sum regions first; they narrow down your domino choices much faster than the smaller targets!",
